    "title": "Gophers meet No. 1-seeded UCLA Friday night in Sweet 16",
    "text": "Minnesota will tip off against No. 1-seeded UCLA with a chance to reach the Elite Eight for the first time since 2004. SACRAMENTO, Calif. — When senior guard Amaya Battle, a former Hopkins High School star, stepped back and drained a jumper just before the buzzer Sunday, Williams Arena erupted. For the first time since 2005, and fourth time in program history, the University of Minnesota women's basketball team was headed to the Sweet 16. Battle is one of several Minnesotans, including Tori McKinney, Mara Braun and Sophie Hart, who have been stepping up to lead the Gophers on their most memorable postseason run in at least two decades. But they're not done. \"We want to keep playing for as long as possible,\" said McKinney, who averages 12.9 points per game this season. \"Although it was our goal to make it to the Sweet 16, now that we're here ... we just want to keep playing for as long as possible. So we're going to go out and compete with everything that we have going to do.\" Minnesota will tip off against No. 1-seeded UCLA Friday night in Sacramento, California, with a chance to reach the Elite Eight for the first time since 2004, when the Lindsay Whalen-led Gophers advanced to their first and only Final Four in program history. \"This has been a journey that's been in the making for quite a few years now for this team,\" said Gophers coach Dawn Plitzuweit. \"For a lot of the young ladies who are a part of this, they've been with us for a few years — obviously have some who joined us just this season — but certainly something that they've worked their tails off to get to a point where they're competing at the level that they are at this point in time.\" The No. 2-ranked Bruins are 21-0 this season against Big Ten teams, including a 76-58 victory over Minnesota on Jan. 15. In that game, Kiki Rice led UCLA with 25 points, and Battle led the Gophers with 16 points. Over the last 10 games, UCLA has averaged 84.2 points, 39.9 rebounds, 22.3 assists, 8.0 steals, and 4.4 blocks per game. Lauren Betts is averaging 17.1 points per game with 8.7 rebounds and 3.2 assists, while Rice is putting up 15 points and 4.9 rebounds per game. \"They're just a great team,\" said Gophers guard Grace Rocholski, who is averaging 12.1 points and 5.1 rebounds this season. \"We've obviously watched them play all season long ... and they really can just do everything, so being able to just make it as tough on them as we can is our goal.\" Minnesota is 8-2 over its last 10 games, with Battle playing some of her best basketball of the year. The senior is averaging 14.3 points, 8.3 rebounds and 3.1 assists over the last 10 games, including a 21-point outing against Green Bay in the opening round of the NCAA tournament. The Gophers are averaging 72.9 points over the last 10 contests, while shooting 44.6% from the field. They've also registered 34.4 rebounds, 16.1 assists and 7.5 steals in that stretch. \"We have a great challenge ahead of us, certainly, with our matchup with UCLA,\" Plitzuweit said, \"but we're really thrilled to have an opportunity to continue playing.\" Tip-off is scheduled for 6:30 p.m. and will be aired on ESPN. ___ The Associated Press contributed to this story",
